</style>
<div class="etb">
<!-- ① TITLE CARD -->
<div class="etb-title-card">
<div class="etb-title-watermark">
7
</div>
<div class="etb-title-unit">
Unit 1 · How Markets Coordinate
</div>
<h2 class="etb-title-main">
Distortions I:
<br>
Taxes, Tariffs, and Tax Incidence
</h2>
<p class="etb-title-sub">
Plucking the Goose
</p>
<div class="etb-title-meta">
<span>
Chapter 7
</span>
<span>
~ 56 min read
</span>
</div>
</div>
<!-- ② HOOK -->
<div class="etb-hook">
<p>
In 1990, Congress decided the rich should pay more, and aimed carefully. It placed a 10% luxury tax on yachts over $100,000, plus private planes, furs, and jewels — goods only the wealthy buy. The logic seemed airtight: tax things only rich people purchase, and only rich people bear the tax. Senators crowed about fairness. The projected haul was tens of millions a year.
</p>
<p>
What happened instead is one of the most instructive accidents in the history of tax policy. The wealthy, it turned out, did not have to buy new American yachts. They bought boats abroad, bought used, or simply didn't buy — their demand was
<em>
elastic
</em>
, full of escape routes. So yacht sales collapsed by roughly three-quarters. But the boat
<em>
builders
</em>
— the welders and carpenters in Maine and Florida — had nowhere to go. Their skills, tools, and shipyards couldn't be redirected overnight; their side of the market was
<em>
inelastic
</em>
. Within a year, thousands of them lost their jobs. A tax aimed at the rich landed on the workers, raised almost no money, and was repealed in 1993.
</p>
<p>
This is the chapter's whole lesson in one cautionary tale:
<strong>
the burden of a tax has almost nothing to do with who legally pays it, and almost everything to do with who can escape it.
</strong>
Whoever is stuck — whoever can't substitute away — bears the load, no matter whose name is on the check. By the end you'll be able to compute exactly how a tax splits between buyers and sellers, why elasticity decides it, and how much value the tax destroys along the way.
</p>
</div>
<!-- ③ CORE IDEA -->
<div class="etb-slbl">
The big picture
</div>
<div class="etb-bigidea">
A tax is a price, and a price is an incentive: it makes the taxed thing more expensive, so people do less of it, driving a wedge between what buyers pay and what sellers keep and shrinking the quantity traded. Who actually bears it — the
<em>
incidence
</em>
— depends not on whom the law taxes but on the relative elasticities of supply and demand: the side that can more easily walk away pays less, the side that's stuck pays more. The tax raises revenue (a rectangle) but also destroys gains from trade that now never happen (a deadweight-loss triangle). The same logic runs tariffs and import quotas (a tax on imports, and its quantity-twin) and subsidies (a tax with the sign flipped). All of this is positive economics — what a tax
<em>
does
</em>
; whether a given tax is
<em>
fair
</em>
is a separate, normative question the model can't settle. By the end you should be able to find the burden on each side, compute revenue and deadweight loss, analyze a tariff and a quota, and read incidence off relative elasticities.
</div>
<!-- ④ VOCABULARY -->
<div class="etb-slbl">
Essential vocabulary — tap each card to reveal
</div>
<div class="etb-flipgrid">
<div class="etb-flip" tabindex="0">
<div class="etb-flip-front">
<div class="etb-flip-front-term">
Tax incidence
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-front-hint">
tap to reveal ↓
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-back">
<div class="etb-flip-back-def">
Who actually bears the burden of a tax — how it splits between buyers and sellers — regardless of who legally hands the money to the government.
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-back-label">
tap to hide ↑
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-flip" tabindex="0">
<div class="etb-flip-front">
<div class="etb-flip-front-term">
Tax wedge
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-front-hint">
tap to reveal ↓
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-back">
<div class="etb-flip-back-def">
The gap a tax opens between the price buyers pay and the price sellers receive. Its size is the tax per unit.
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-back-label">
tap to hide ↑
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-flip" tabindex="0">
<div class="etb-flip-front">
<div class="etb-flip-front-term">
Legal vs. economic incidence
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-front-hint">
tap to reveal ↓
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-back">
<div class="etb-flip-back-def">
Legal incidence is who the law requires to pay; economic incidence is who really bears the cost. They are usually different — economics decides the second, not the statute.
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-back-label">
tap to hide ↑
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-flip" tabindex="0">
<div class="etb-flip-front">
<div class="etb-flip-front-term">
Elasticity
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-front-hint">
tap to reveal ↓
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-back">
<div class="etb-flip-back-def">
How responsive buyers or sellers are to a price change — how easily they can walk away. The more elastic side has escape routes; the inelastic side is stuck.
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-back-label">
tap to hide ↑
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-flip" tabindex="0">
<div class="etb-flip-front">
<div class="etb-flip-front-term">
Tax revenue
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-front-hint">
tap to reveal ↓
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-back">
<div class="etb-flip-back-def">
The money the government collects: the tax per unit times the quantity still traded. On the graph, a rectangle (tax × quantity).
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-back-label">
tap to hide ↑
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-flip" tabindex="0">
<div class="etb-flip-front">
<div class="etb-flip-front-term">
Tariff
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-front-hint">
tap to reveal ↓
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-back">
<div class="etb-flip-back-def">
A tax on imported goods. Mechanically just a commodity tax applied at the border — same wedge, same incidence logic, same deadweight loss.
</div>
<div class="etb-flip-back-label">
tap to hide ↑
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-slbl">
Also know — tap any term
</div>
<div class="etb-alsoknow">
<span class="etb-ak-item">
excise tax
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
A per-unit tax on a specific good (gasoline, cigarettes, yachts) — the kind analyzed here.
</span>
</span>
<span class="etb-ak-item">
deadweight loss
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
Value destroyed when a tax stops trades that would have happened. From Ch 6 — now caused by a tax.
</span>
</span>
<span class="etb-ak-item">
protectionism
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
Shielding domestic producers from foreign competition, usually via tariffs or quotas. Bastiat's target.
</span>
</span>
<span class="etb-ak-item">
pass-through
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
The share of a tax that gets passed on to buyers as a higher price.
</span>
</span>
<span class="etb-ak-item">
subsidy
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
A payment that lowers price or cost — a tax with the sign flipped. The whole of Ch 8.
</span>
</span>
<span class="etb-ak-item">
distortion
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
Anything that pushes a market away from its efficient equilibrium, shrinking total surplus.
</span>
</span>
<span class="etb-ak-item">
world price
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
The price at which a country can buy or sell a good on the global market — the baseline free trade sets.
</span>
</span>
<span class="etb-ak-item">
import quota
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
A legal cap on the quantity of a good that may be imported — the quantity-side twin of a tariff.
</span>
</span>
<span class="etb-ak-item">
quota rents
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
The extra value created by a quota's price gap, captured by whoever holds the import licenses rather than the government.
</span>
</span>
<span class="etb-ak-item">
Bastiat
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
19th-century French economist whose satirical "Candlemakers' Petition" skewered protectionism.
</span>
</span>
<span class="etb-ak-item">
positive economics
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
Claims about what
<em>
is
</em>
— describable, testable. "A tax falls on the inelastic side" is positive.
</span>
</span>
<span class="etb-ak-item">
normative economics
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
Claims about what
<em>
ought
</em>
to be — value judgments. "This tax is fair" is normative; the model can't settle it.
</span>
</span>
<span class="etb-ak-item">
Laffer curve
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
Revenue rises then falls as the tax rate climbs — zero at 0% and zero at 100% — so a revenue-maximizing rate sits somewhere between.
</span>
</span>
<span class="etb-ak-item">
progressive tax
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
Takes a larger share of income as income rises (e.g. the federal income tax with rising brackets).
</span>
</span>
<span class="etb-ak-item">
regressive tax
<span class="etb-ak-pop">
Takes a larger share of income as income falls (e.g. a flat sales tax, since the poor spend a bigger share of income).
</span>
</span>
</div>
<!-- ⑤ CONCEPT 1 — THE WEDGE & INCIDENCE -->
<div class="etb-concept">
<div class="etb-slbl">
Major concept 1 · A Tax splits the market, and the law can't say how
</div>
<h2>
The Tax Wedge
</h2>
<div class="etb-body">
<p>
A
<strong>
tax is a price, and a price is an incentive.
</strong>
Tax something and you make it more expensive, so people do less of it. Ronald Reagan once mocked how readily governments reach for that lever:
</p>
<blockquote class="etb-quote">
Government's view of the economy could be summed up in a few short phrases: If it moves, tax it. If it keeps moving, regulate it. And if it stops moving, subsidize it.
<span class="etb-quote-attr">
— Ronald Reagan, remarks to the White House Conference on Small Business, 1986 (delivered, to laughter, as a jab at over-government)
</span>
</blockquote>
<p>
It was a partisan applause line, not a neutral description — but the mechanics underneath are real and apply to any tax. A tax discourages whatever it touches. Usually that discouragement is an unwanted side effect: we tax cigarettes mostly for revenue and get less smoking, we tax income and get a little less work. Occasionally the discouragement is the whole point — taxing things we'd like less of — an idea we'll sharpen later. Either way the quantity shrinks, which is exactly the wedge we're about to draw. And the flip side of Reagan's line — "subsidize it" — is the mirror image, a tax with the sign reversed, waiting in the next chapter.
</p>
<p>
So, the wedge. Picture any market at its equilibrium, the one we measured in Chapter 6.
<span class="etb-xref">
Ch 6
</span>
Now the government places a tax of, say, $15 on each unit. The immediate effect is a
<span data-tip="The gap a tax opens between what buyers pay and what sellers keep.">
tax wedge
</span>
: the price the buyer pays and the price the seller keeps can no longer be the same number, because $15 of every transaction now goes to the government. The buyer pays one price; the seller pockets that price minus $15. The market settles at a smaller quantity, because some trades that barely made sense before don't survive the wedge.
</p>
<p>
Here's the first surprise, and it's the heart of the chapter. It does not matter whether the law taxes the seller or the buyer — the outcome is identical. Tax the seller $15 per unit and they try to charge more; tax the buyer $15 and they offer sellers less. Either way the same wedge opens, the same quantity trades, and the same split of the burden emerges. The
<span data-tip="Who the law says must pay.">
legal incidence
</span>
— whose name is on the check — has no effect on the
<span data-tip="Who actually bears the cost after prices adjust.">
economic incidence
</span>
, who actually ends up worse off. As economists put it: who pays the tax is decided not by the laws of Congress but by the laws of supply and demand.
</p>
</div>
<div class="etb-figure">
<div class="etb-svgwrap">
<svg viewBox="0 0 680 400" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="A per-unit tax shifts supply up, opening a wedge between the price buyers pay and the price sellers receive, reducing quantity">
<line x1="70" y1="50" x2="70" y2="350" stroke="#1a1a1a" stroke-width="1.5">
