Price ($)
</text>
<rect x="70" y="175" fill="#c48b00" opacity="0.22">
</rect>
<line x1="70" y1="100" x2="600" y2="350" stroke="#a33030" stroke-width="2.5">
</line>
<text x="600" y="345" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="#a33030">
D
</text>
<line x1="70" y1="250" x2="600" y2="125" stroke="#0f7d7d" stroke-width="2.5">
</line>
<text x="604" y="123" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="#0f7d7d">
S
</text>
<line x1="70" y1="212.5" x2="547" y2="100" stroke="#0f7d7d" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 3">
</line>
<text x="551" y="98" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#0f7d7d">
S+tax
</text>
<circle cx="282" cy="200" r="4" fill="#1a3a5c" opacity="0.5">
</circle>
<line x1="229" y1="175" x2="229" y2="350" stroke="#1a1a1a" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3">
</line>
<line x1="70" y1="175" x2="229" y2="175" stroke="#a33030" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3">
</line>
<line x1="70" y1="212.5" x2="229" y2="212.5" stroke="#0f7d7d" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3">
</line>
<circle cx="229" cy="175" r="4.5" fill="#a33030">
</circle>
<circle cx="229" cy="212.5" r="4.5" fill="#0f7d7d">
</circle>
<text x="237" y="171" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" fill="#a33030">
buyers pay $70
</text>
<text x="237" y="226" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" fill="#0a4a4a">
sellers keep $55
</text>
<text x="300" y="200" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#8a6d2f">
tax = $15 wedge
</text>
<text x="135" y="170" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="9.5" font-weight="700" fill="#8a6d2f">
tax revenue
</text>
<text x="229" y="365" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10.5" fill="#777" text-anchor="middle">
15
</text>
<text x="282" y="365" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10.5" fill="#777" text-anchor="middle" opacity="0.6">
20
</text>
</svg>
</div>
<div class="etb-figure-cap">
<span class="etb-figure-num">
Fig 7.1
</span>
<span class="etb-figure-title">
A $15 tax on the Chapter 6 market (demand P = 100 − 2Q, supply P = 40 + Q). Quantity falls from 20 to 15; buyers pay $70, sellers keep $55.
</span>
<div class="etb-figure-notice">
<strong>
What to notice:
</strong>
the tax lifts the price buyers pay by only $10 (60 → 70) but cuts what sellers keep by $5 (60 → 55). The $15 wedge is split — and it's split unevenly. Why unevenly is the next concept.
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-worked">
<div class="etb-worked-header">
<span class="etb-worked-badge">
Worked example
</span>
<span class="etb-worked-title">
Finding the burden on each side
</span>
</div>
<div class="etb-worked-body">
<p>
Market: demand P = 100 − 2Q, supply P = 40 + Q (equilibrium P = $60, Q = 20). Impose a $15 per-unit tax. Find the new quantity, the price buyers pay, the price sellers keep, and each side's share of the tax.
</p>
<ol class="etb-worked-steps">
<li>
A tax of $15 lifts the price sellers need by $15 at every quantity: the effective supply becomes P = 40 + Q + 15 = 55 + Q.
</li>
<li>
Find the new quantity where buyers' price meets sellers'-with-tax: 100 − 2Q = 55 + Q → 45 = 3Q → Q = 15.
</li>
<li>
Price buyers pay (on the demand curve): P = 100 − 2(15) = $70. Price sellers keep: $70 − $15 tax = $55.
</li>
<li>
Split the $15 wedge against the old $60 price: buyers bear 70 − 60 = $10; sellers bear 60 − 55 = $5. (Check: 10 + 5 = 15. ✓)
</li>
</ol>
<div class="etb-worked-answer">
New quantity 15; buyers pay $70, sellers keep $55. Buyers bear $10 of the tax, sellers $5 — even if the law put the whole tax on the sellers. The check-writer is not the burden-bearer.
</div>
</div>
</div>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Quick check
</span>
A law says sellers must pay a $4 tax. After prices adjust, buyers pay $3 more than before. How much of the tax do sellers actually bear?
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
$1. The tax is $4; buyers bear $3 of it through the higher price, so sellers bear the remaining $1 — even though the law named the sellers.
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Quick check
</span>
Solo: Demand P = 120 − 3Q, supply P = 20 + Q, with a $12 per-unit tax. Find the new quantity and the price buyers pay. (Hint: add the tax to the supply intercept.)
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Supply-with-tax: P = 32 + Q. Set 120 − 3Q = 32 + Q → 88 = 4Q → Q = 22. Buyers pay P = 120 − 3(22) = $54. (Sellers keep $54 − $12 = $42.)
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-retrieval">
<summary>
<span class="etb-ret-badge">
Retrieval practice
</span>
Why does it make no difference whether the law taxes buyers or sellers?
<span class="etb-ret-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-ret-body">
<p>
Because a tax changes the
<em>
gap
</em>
between what buyers pay and what sellers receive, and that gap is the same wedge no matter which side hands over the money. Tax the seller, and they need a higher price to clear the same quantity, so the price buyers face rises; tax the buyer, and they offer sellers less, so the price sellers receive falls. Run either story to its end and you reach the identical new quantity, buyer price, and seller price. What determines how the wedge
<em>
splits
</em>
isn't the statute — it's how easily each side can walk away, which is elasticity. The statute picks who writes the check; supply and demand pick who feels the cost. A wrong answer usually assumes "taxing the seller means the seller pays," which confuses legal incidence with economic incidence — the single most common tax mistake there is.
</p>
</div>
</details>
</div>
<!-- ⑤ CONCEPT 2 — ELASTICITY DECIDES -->
<div class="etb-concept">
<div class="etb-slbl">
Major concept 2 · Who can't escape
</div>
<h2>
Elasticity decides the burden
</h2>
<div class="etb-body">
<p>
If the law doesn't decide who bears a tax, what does? The answer is the most useful idea in this chapter:
<strong>
the burden falls on whichever side can least escape it.
</strong>
"Escape" is just
<span data-tip="How easily buyers or sellers can change their behavior when price changes.">
elasticity
</span>
in plain clothes. An elastic side has alternatives — substitutes to buy instead, other markets to sell into — so when a tax pushes the price the wrong way for them, they simply do less of it and dodge most of the burden. An inelastic side is stuck: no good substitutes, no easy exit, so they swallow the tax to keep trading.
</p>
<p>
What makes one side elastic and the other stuck? A handful of things, and you can feel every one of them without touching a number. Demand is more
<em>
elastic
</em>
when buyers have somewhere to go: close substitutes exist (one cola with another right beside it on the shelf), the purchase is a discretionary luxury rather than a necessity, it eats a big share of the budget so a price jump really stings, it's defined narrowly ("a Honda Civic" has substitutes; "a car" has almost none), or buyers have time to adjust. Flip each of those and demand turns
<em>
inelastic
</em>
— no substitutes, a necessity, a trivial share of spending, broadly defined, no time to react. The supply side runs on the same logic: sellers are elastic when they can redeploy easily (spare capacity, time, transferable skills) and inelastic when they're stuck (a built shipyard, land that can't be moved).
</p>
</div>
<div class="etb-2col">
<div class="etb-2item">
<div class="etb-2item-hd">
More elastic — easy to escape
</div>
<div class="etb-2item-bd">
Close substitutes available · a luxury you can skip · a big share of your budget · narrowly defined good · plenty of time to adjust · sellers with spare capacity or transferable skills.
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-2item">
<div class="etb-2item-hd">
More inelastic — stuck
</div>
<div class="etb-2item-bd">
No good substitutes · a necessity (or addictive) · a tiny share of spending · broadly defined good · no time to react · sellers who can't redeploy (land, a built factory).
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-body">
<p>
Train the reflex. For each good below, decide
<em>
elastic
</em>
or
<em>
inelastic
</em>
— and why — before you tap.
</p>
</div>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Elastic or inelastic?
</span>
Table salt
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Inelastic. No real substitute, it's a tiny share of anyone's budget, and you buy about the same amount whatever the price. Salt could double and your salt-buying would barely move.
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Elastic or inelastic?
</span>
One particular brand of bottled water, with ten others on the same shelf
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Elastic. The defining feature is close substitutes sitting right next to it — raise that one brand's price and buyers simply grab the bottle beside it. (Note "bottled water" in general is far less elastic than "this brand of bottled water." Narrow vs. broad.)
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Elastic or inelastic?
</span>
Insulin, for someone who needs it to live
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Extremely inelastic. A necessity with no substitute and life-or-death stakes — quantity barely responds to price. This is why a tax (or a price hike) on insulin lands squarely on patients: they can't walk away.
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Elastic or inelastic?
</span>
Dinners out at restaurants
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Elastic. A discretionary luxury with an easy substitute — cooking at home — and often a real chunk of a budget. When prices rise, people cut back fast.
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Elastic or inelastic?
</span>
Gasoline next week vs. gasoline over the next ten years
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Inelastic next week (you still have to drive to work; no time to change anything), but much more elastic over a decade (you can buy a fuel-efficient car, move closer to work, switch to transit). Same good — the time horizon flips the elasticity. That's the time-to-adjust rule.
</div>
</details>
<div class="etb-body">
<p>
This is centuries-old wisdom. The line attributed to Jean-Baptiste Colbert, Louis XIV's finance minister, is that
<span data-tip="Traditionally attributed to Colbert (c. 1665–83); the documentary trail is thin, but the attribution is conventional.">
"the art of taxation consists in so plucking the goose as to obtain the largest amount of feathers with the least possible amount of hissing."
</span>
Strip away the cynicism and it's an elasticity rule in disguise: pluck where there's least hissing — tax the side that can't fly off. The goose that can escape (the elastic side) hisses loudly and loses few feathers; the goose that's stuck (the inelastic side) is plucked bare. The reason a luxury-yacht tax fails and a gasoline tax succeeds in raising revenue is the same reason: one goose can fly, the other can't.
</p>
<p>
That's the whole yacht story. The wealthy buyers were
<em>
elastic
</em>
— boats abroad, used boats, no boat at all — so they escaped the tax by not buying. The boat
<em>
builders
</em>
were
<em>
inelastic
</em>
— their shipyards and skills couldn't pivot to making something else — so the burden rolled downhill onto them as lost work. The rule, worth memorizing:
<strong>
the more elastic side pays a smaller share of the tax; the more inelastic side pays a larger share.
</strong>
Think of relative elasticity as relative ability to flee. Whoever is more stuck, pays more.
</p>
<p>
You can even read it off the graph without the rule. In our anchor market, demand is steeper (slope −2) than supply is shallow (slope +1) — the steeper curve is the less elastic one, so buyers are more stuck, and indeed buyers bore $10 of the $15 tax while sellers bore only $5. The numbers and the intuition agree: the inelastic side carries more. (We're working from the
<em>
intuition
</em>
of elasticity here — who's stuck. The number that makes it precise gets its own chapter later.)
</p>
</div>
<table class="etb-compare">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>
If this side is more inelastic…
</th>
<th>
…they bear more of the tax, because
</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>
Buyers (inelastic demand — few substitutes, e.g. insulin, gasoline)
</td>
<td>
they keep buying even as the price rises, so the tax passes through to them as a higher price.
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
Sellers (inelastic supply — can't redeploy, e.g. boat builders, landowners)
</td>
<td>
they keep selling even as their take falls, so the tax comes out of their pocket as a lower price received.
</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Quick check
</span>
A tax is placed on a good with very inelastic demand (insulin) and elastic supply. Who bears most of the tax?
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Buyers. With inelastic demand they can't easily stop buying, so they're stuck with the burden; the elastic sellers escape by redeploying elsewhere.
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Quick check
</span>
Solo: A town taxes hotel rooms. Tourists have many nearby towns to choose from (elastic demand); hotels can't move their buildings (inelastic supply). Who bears more of the tax, and why?
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
The hotels (sellers). Tourists escape by staying elsewhere, so demand is elastic; the buildings are stuck, so supply is inelastic. The inelastic, stuck side — the hotels — bears the larger share.
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Quick check
</span>
Back to the hook: the luxury tax was meant to fall on rich buyers but landed on boat workers. Translate that into elasticity language.
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Rich buyers had elastic demand (buy abroad, used, or not at all) so they escaped; boat builders had inelastic supply (skills and shipyards couldn't redeploy) so they were stuck and bore the burden as lost jobs.
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-retrieval">
<summary>
<span class="etb-ret-badge">
Retrieval practice
</span>
Explain the principle "tax the inelastic side" to someone designing a tax to actually fall on the wealthy.
<span class="etb-ret-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-ret-body">
<p>
The burden of a tax lands on whoever can't escape it, and "escape" means having alternatives — elasticity. To make a tax actually fall on the wealthy, you'd need to tax something for which the wealthy have
<em>
inelastic
</em>
demand or supply: something they can't easily substitute away from, buy elsewhere, or stop doing. Luxury yachts failed precisely because rich buyers had endless escape routes (foreign boats, used boats, no boat), so their elastic demand dodged the tax and it rolled onto the inelastic boat builders. A tax on, say, land or very high incomes is harder to escape, which is exactly why economists tend to think those fall closer to their intended target. The deep point: good tax design starts by asking "which side here is stuck?" — because that's the side that will pay, whatever the law says.
<span class="etb-xref">
Ch 8
</span>
</p>
</div>
</details>
</div>
<!-- ⑤ CONCEPT 3 — REVENUE & DWL -->
<div class="etb-concept">
<div class="etb-slbl">
Major concept 3 · The cost of the wedge
</div>
<h2>
Revenue and deadweight loss
</h2>
<div class="etb-body">
<p>
A tax does two things at once, and it's vital to keep them separate. First, it
<em>
raises revenue
</em>
: the government collects the tax on every unit still sold. That's a transfer — surplus moving from buyers and sellers to the public purse, to fund roads or schools or whatever the state does. On the graph it's a
<strong>
rectangle
</strong>
: the tax per unit (the height of the wedge) times the quantity still traded (the width). In our market, $15 × 15 units = $225 of revenue.
</p>
<p>
Second, and more subtly, a tax
<em>
destroys
</em>
value. Some trades that used to happen — where the buyer valued the good above the seller's cost — no longer clear once the wedge is driven in. Those gains from trade simply vanish, captured by no one, not even the government. This is
<span data-tip="Value destroyed when a tax stops worthwhile trades.">
deadweight loss
</span>
, the same triangle from Chapter 6, now caused by a tax.
<span class="etb-xref">
Ch 6
</span>
It sits between supply and demand, from the new quantity out to the old one.
</p>
</div>
<div class="etb-worked">
<div class="etb-worked-header">
<span class="etb-worked-badge">
Worked example
</span>
<span class="etb-worked-title">
Tax revenue (rectangle) and deadweight loss (triangle)
</span>
</div>
<div class="etb-worked-body">
<p>
Same market and $15 tax: quantity fell from 20 to 15. Find the tax revenue and the deadweight loss.
</p>
<ol class="etb-worked-steps">
<li>
<strong>
Revenue (rectangle):
</strong>
tax per unit × quantity sold = $15 × 15 = $225.
</li>
<li>
<strong>
Deadweight loss (triangle):
</strong>
the base is the lost trades, 20 − 15 = 5; the height is the tax wedge, $15.
</li>
<li>
Area = ½ × base × height = ½ × 5 × 15 = $37.50.
</li>
</ol>
<div class="etb-worked-answer">
Revenue = $225 (a transfer, still exists). Deadweight loss = $37.50 (gone for good). The deadweight-loss triangle always sits between the curves, from the taxed quantity out to the efficient one — the trades the wedge killed.
</div>
</div>
</div>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Quick check
</span>
Solo: A $6 tax cuts quantity from 100 to 90. Find tax revenue and deadweight loss.
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Revenue = $6 × 90 = $540. DWL = ½ × (100 − 90) × 6 = ½ × 10 × 6 = $30.
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Quick check
</span>
Why isn't the tax revenue itself a "loss" to society the way deadweight loss is?
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Revenue is a transfer — it moves surplus from buyers and sellers to the government, which spends it. The value still exists. Deadweight loss is value that stops existing because the trade never happens; no one gets it.
</div>
</details>
<div class="etb-body">
<p>
Here the taxes-as-incentive idea bites the government back. Point the incentive at the tax
<em>
rate
</em>
itself: if a higher rate collects more per unit, why not keep raising it forever? Because the rate is also an incentive to shrink the very thing being taxed. At a 0% rate the government collects nothing — obviously. But at a 100% rate it also collects almost nothing: if the state takes every dollar, almost no one bothers to earn, sell, or report the dollar in the first place, so the base all but vanishes. Zero at one end, near-zero at the other — so somewhere between, revenue must peak. Plot revenue against the rate and you get a hump: the
<span data-tip="Named for economist Arthur Laffer, who popularized it in the 1970s; the underlying idea is older.">
Laffer curve
</span>
.
</p>
</div>
<div class="etb-figure">
<div class="etb-svgwrap">
<svg viewBox="0 0 680 360" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="The Laffer curve: tax revenue rises then falls as the tax rate climbs from 0 to 100 percent, peaking at a disputed point in the middle">
<line x1="70" y1="40" x2="70" y2="320" stroke="#1a1a1a" stroke-width="1.5">
</line>
<line x1="70" y1="320" x2="620" y2="320" stroke="#1a1a1a" stroke-width="1.5">
</line>
<text x="345" y="352" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" fill="#1a3a5c" text-anchor="middle">
Tax rate
</text>
<text x="30" y="180" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" fill="#1a3a5c" text-anchor="middle" transform="rotate(-90 30 180)">
Tax revenue
</text>
<text x="70" y="336" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#777" text-anchor="middle">
0%
</text>
<text x="620" y="336" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#777" text-anchor="middle">
100%
</text>
<path d="M 70 320 Q 345 -40 620 320" fill="none" stroke="#1a3a5c" stroke-width="2.5">
</path>
<line x1="345" y1="140" x2="345" y2="320" stroke="#1a1a1a" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3">
</line>
<circle cx="345" cy="140" r="5" fill="#c48b00">
</circle>
<text x="345" y="130" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#8a6d2f" text-anchor="middle">
peak — where? (disputed)
</text>
<text x="150" y="250" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10.5" fill="#2a7a4f">
raise rate → revenue up
</text>
<text x="540" y="250" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10.5" fill="#a33030" text-anchor="end">
raise rate → base shrinks → revenue down
</text>
</svg>
</div>
<div class="etb-figure-cap">
<span class="etb-figure-num">
Fig 7.2
</span>
<span class="etb-figure-title">
The Laffer curve. Revenue is zero at a 0% rate and near zero at 100%, so it must peak somewhere between — but exactly where is hotly disputed.
</span>
<div class="etb-figure-notice">
<strong>
What to notice:
</strong>
the hump itself is guaranteed by the two endpoints; the peak's
<em>
location
</em>
is not. "Cut taxes to raise revenue" only works on the right-hand downslope — and for most real taxes we sit on the left.
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-research">
<div class="etb-research-header">
<span class="etb-research-badge">
Where the evidence is live
</span>
<span class="etb-research-title">
Which side of the hump are we on?
</span>
</div>
<div class="etb-research-body">
<p>
The
<em>
shape
</em>
isn't controversial — zero at both ends guarantees a peak. What's fiercely disputed is
<em>
where
</em>
the peak sits, and that's where careful economics and political slogans part ways. "Cut tax rates and revenue will rise" is true only if you're already past the peak, on the downward slope. For most taxes at the rates rich countries actually use, the weight of evidence is that we sit on the
<em>
upward
</em>
side, where cutting rates loses revenue rather than raising it. Estimates of the revenue-maximizing top income-tax rate land high and disagree with one another (often cited loosely in the 60–70% neighborhood), precisely because the answer turns on how easily people can shelter income or stop earning — elasticity again. The honest takeaway: the curve is real and a peak exists, but "tax cuts pay for themselves" is a claim about a specific magnitude that is usually wrong, not a consequence of the diagram. Be wary of anyone who states the peak's location with confidence in either direction.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Quick check
</span>
A country's tax rate is already extremely high and revenue is falling as it rises further. Would cutting the rate raise or lower revenue?
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Raise it — if the country is genuinely past the peak (on the downslope), a lower rate shrinks the base less, so revenue climbs. The catch: this only holds past the peak. At the low-to-moderate rates most economies actually use, cutting the rate
<em>
lowers
</em>
revenue.
</div>
</details>
</div>
<!-- ⑤ CONCEPT 4 — INTERNATIONAL TRADE: TARIFFS & QUOTAS -->
<div class="etb-concept">
<div class="etb-slbl">
Major concept 4 · The wedge crosses the border
</div>
<h2>
International trade: tariffs and quotas
</h2>
<div class="etb-body">
<p>
Two chapters back you were handed a promise. We said the gut-level horror at "a tax on Vermont maple syrup crossing into New Hampshire" was the very same logic as the case against tariffs
<em>
between nations
</em>
, and that we'd make it precise once we had the welfare tools.
<span class="etb-xref">
Ch 5
</span>
Time to pay that off — and the good news is you already own the machine. A
<span data-tip="A tax on imported goods, collected at the border.">
tariff
</span>
is a tax on imports, and a tax is a wedge. The only genuinely new idea trade adds is a
<span data-tip="The going price on the global market — what a country can buy or sell at.">
world price
</span>
.
</p>
<p>
When a country opens to trade, it faces a world price: the price at which it can buy as much as it wants from the global market. Picture our familiar market — domestic demand P = 100 − 2Q, domestic supply P = 40 + Q — with a world price of $50, comfortably below the $60 this market would reach on its own. At $50, domestic buyers want 25 units, but domestic sellers will only supply 10. The 15-unit gap is filled by imports. Lots of cheap foreign goods; domestic consumers are better off for them. That's free trade, and it's the baseline every trade restriction pushes against.
</p>
<p>
Now the government places a $6 tariff on each imported unit. To foreign sellers this is exactly the per-unit tax from Concept 1: it lifts the price they must clear to $56. Domestic buyers face $56 too, and two things happen at once. Consumers retreat up the demand curve, 25 → 22. And domestic producers, now shielded by the higher price, expand along the supply curve, 10 → 16. Imports get squeezed from both ends: 22 − 16 = 6 units, down from 15.
</p>
<p>
Notice the tariff bends
<em>
two
</em>
margins, not one. A plain domestic tax (Concept 3) distorted only the quantity traded, leaving a single deadweight-loss triangle. A tariff distorts domestic
<em>
production
</em>
(units 10→16 are now made at home at a cost above the world price)
<em>
and
</em>
domestic
<em>
consumption
</em>
(units 22→25 are worth more than the world price but go unbought) — so its deadweight loss is
<strong>
two
</strong>
triangles.
</p>
</div>
<div class="etb-figure">
<div class="etb-svgwrap">
<svg viewBox="0 0 680 400" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="A tariff raises the domestic price from the world price of 50 to 56, expanding domestic production, cutting consumption, shrinking imports, with a government revenue rectangle and two deadweight-loss triangles">
<line x1="70" y1="50" x2="70" y2="350" stroke="#1a1a1a" stroke-width="1.5">
</line>
<line x1="70" y1="350" x2="620" y2="350" stroke="#1a1a1a" stroke-width="1.5">
</line>
