</text>
</svg>
</div>
<div class="etb-figure-cap">
<span class="etb-figure-num">
Fig 7.3
</span>
<span class="etb-figure-title">
A $6 tariff on the anchor market at a $50 world price. Domestic price rises to $56; production 10→16, consumption 25→22, imports 15→6. Green rectangle = tariff revenue ($36); the two gold triangles = deadweight loss ($18 + $9 = $27).
</span>
<div class="etb-figure-notice">
<strong>
What to notice:
</strong>
the rectangle is a transfer (consumers' money to the treasury); the two triangles are pure loss — the left one from over-producing at home, the right one from under-consuming. Hold onto that green rectangle: under a quota it changes hands.
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-worked">
<div class="etb-worked-header">
<span class="etb-worked-badge">
Worked example
</span>
<span class="etb-worked-title">
The welfare cost of a tariff
</span>
</div>
<div class="etb-worked-body">
<p>
Domestic demand P = 100 − 2Q, supply P = 40 + Q, world price $50, tariff $6. Find the domestic price, production, consumption, imports, government revenue, and deadweight loss.
</p>
<ol class="etb-worked-steps">
<li>
<strong>
Free-trade baseline ($50):
</strong>
consumption Q
<sub>
d
</sub>
= (100 − 50)/2 = 25; production Q
<sub>
s
</sub>
= 50 − 40 = 10; imports = 25 − 10 = 15.
</li>
<li>
<strong>
With the tariff, price = 50 + 6 = $56.
</strong>
Consumption Q
<sub>
d
</sub>
= (100 − 56)/2 = 22; production Q
<sub>
s
</sub>
= 56 − 40 = 16; imports = 22 − 16 = 6.
</li>
<li>
<strong>
Government revenue (rectangle):
</strong>
tariff × imports = $6 × 6 = $36.
</li>
<li>
<strong>
Deadweight loss (two triangles):
</strong>
production side ½ × (16 − 10) × 6 = ½ × 6 × 6 = $18; consumption side ½ × (25 − 22) × 6 = ½ × 3 × 6 = $9.
</li>
</ol>
<div class="etb-worked-answer">
Price $56; production 16, consumption 22, imports 6; revenue $36; deadweight loss = 18 + 9 = $27. Domestic producers win and the treasury collects — but consumers lose more than those two gains combined, and the difference is the $27 destroyed outright.
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-body">
<p>
The full ledger makes the "poorer on net" claim concrete. Consumers lose the most; producers and the government gain back part of it; the gap that no one recovers is exactly the two triangles:
</p>
</div>
<table class="etb-compare">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>
Who
</th>
<th>
Change vs. free trade
</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>
Consumers
</td>
<td>
−$141 (pay $6 more on 22 units, plus the 3 units they no longer buy)
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
Domestic producers
</td>
<td>
+$78 (sell at $6 more, and 6 more units)
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
Government
</td>
<td>
+$36 (tariff revenue)
</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>
Net to the country
</td>
<td>
−$27 = the deadweight loss ($18 + $9)
</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="etb-body">
<p>
Now the quota. An
<span data-tip="A legal cap on how much may be imported.">
import quota
</span>
attacks the same market from the quantity side: rather than tax imports, the law simply caps them — "no more than 6 imported units, full stop." With only 6 units coming in, domestic buyers and sellers must close the rest of the gap themselves, and the domestic price climbs until the home shortage is exactly 6. That price turns out to be… $56 — the very same number the tariff produced. Same price, same 22 consumed and 16 produced, same two deadweight-loss triangles. The tariff and the quota are twins.
</p>
<p>
They differ in exactly one place: that green rectangle. Under the tariff, the $6 gap on each of the 6 imported units — $36 — is collected by the government as revenue. Under the quota, the government collects nothing. Yet the gap still exists: someone is buying at the $50 world price and selling at $56. That $36 is real, and it goes to whoever holds the import licenses as
<span data-tip="The price gap a quota opens, captured by license holders rather than the treasury.">
quota rents
</span>
. If domestic importers hold the licenses, the $36 stays in the country and the quota costs the nation the same $27 as the tariff. If foreign exporters capture the licenses — common when quotas are administered abroad — the $36 leaves the country too, and the quota is
<em>
strictly worse
</em>
than the equivalent tariff.
</p>
</div>
<div class="etb-worked">
<div class="etb-worked-header">
<span class="etb-worked-badge">
Worked example
</span>
<span class="etb-worked-title">
The same market under an import quota
</span>
</div>
<div class="etb-worked-body">
<p>
Same market and $50 world price. Instead of a tariff, the government sets an import quota of 6 units. Find the domestic price, the deadweight loss, and who gets the rectangle.
</p>
<ol class="etb-worked-steps">
<li>
The domestic price rises until the home shortage equals the quota: Q
<sub>
d
</sub>
− Q
<sub>
s
</sub>
= 6. With Q
<sub>
d
</sub>
= (100 − P)/2 and Q
<sub>
s
</sub>
= P − 40: (50 − P/2) − (P − 40) = 6 → 90 − 1.5P = 6 → P = $56.
</li>
<li>
At $56: consumption 22, production 16, imports 22 − 16 = 6 ✓ (the cap binds exactly). Identical to the tariff outcome.
</li>
<li>
<strong>
Deadweight loss:
</strong>
the same two triangles — $18 + $9 = $27.
</li>
<li>
<strong>
The rectangle:
</strong>
price gap × imports = ($56 − $50) × 6 = $36 — but now it's quota rents to license holders, not government revenue.
</li>
</ol>
<div class="etb-worked-answer">
Price $56, deadweight loss $27 — identical to the $6 tariff. The only difference is the $36: revenue for the treasury under the tariff, rents for license holders under the quota. If those holders are foreign, the country loses the $36 as well, making the quota the costlier tool.
</div>
</div>
</div>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Quick check
</span>
A tariff and an import quota are set so that each allows the same number of imports. Which one raises the domestic price more?
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Neither — they raise it by exactly the same amount. That's the equivalence: matched to allow the same imports, a tariff and a quota produce identical price, quantities, and deadweight loss. They differ only in who pockets the rectangle.
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Quick check
</span>
Solo: Domestic demand P = 80 − Q, supply P = 20 + Q, world price $40, tariff $5. Find the domestic price, imports after the tariff, government revenue, and deadweight loss.
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Price = 40 + 5 = $45. At $45: Q
<sub>
d
</sub>
= 80 − 45 = 35, Q
<sub>
s
</sub>
= 45 − 20 = 25, imports = 10. Free trade at $40: Q
<sub>
d
</sub>
= 40, Q
<sub>
s
</sub>
= 20, imports = 20. Revenue = $5 × 10 = $50. DWL = production ½ × (25 − 20) × 5 = $12.50, plus consumption ½ × (40 − 35) × 5 = $12.50, total $25.
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Quick check
</span>
Why might a government prefer a tariff to an otherwise-identical quota — and why might a domestic industry lobby for the quota instead?
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
The government prefers the tariff because it collects the rectangle as revenue; under a quota that money becomes rents it doesn't get. A domestic industry may push for a quota precisely to capture those rents (if it holds the licenses) and because a hard quantity cap protects it even if foreign costs fall — a tariff still lets cheaper imports in.
</div>
</details>
<div class="etb-body">
<p>
This was Bastiat's joke in 1845. In his satirical
<em>
Candlemakers' Petition
</em>
, France's candlemakers beg the government to block out the sun — that "unfair foreign competitor" flooding the market with free light — so domestic lighting can flourish. The absurdity is the point: protecting producers from cheaper competition always costs consumers more than it saves the producers. A tariff on sunlight, on syrup, or on steel — or a quota on any of them — follows the same arithmetic. The protected producers are the
<em>
seen
</em>
; the dispersed consumers paying more are the
<em>
unseen
</em>
.
</p>
</div>
<details class="etb-misc" open="">
<summary>
<span class="etb-misc-badge">
Common misconception
</span>
"A quota is gentler than a tariff — there's no tax, so consumers don't pay."
<span class="etb-misc-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-misc-body">
<p>
Consumers pay exactly the same under both. A binding quota drives the domestic price up by precisely the amount a matched tariff would — in our case, to $56 either way — so consumers face the identical higher price and buy the identical smaller quantity. "No tax" is true only in the narrow sense that no money goes to the treasury; the price hike, the lost consumption, and the two deadweight-loss triangles are all still there. The single real difference is the rectangle: under a tariff the government collects it; under a quota it's captured as rents by whoever holds the import licenses — sometimes foreign firms, in which case the quota is the
<em>
more
</em>
expensive choice for the country, not the gentler one.
</p>
</div>
</details>
<div class="etb-note">
<div class="etb-note-label">
Two families of inefficiency — a map for the rest of the book
</div>
<div class="etb-note-body">
<p>
Step back and notice what we've now collected. Price ceilings and floors
<span class="etb-xref">
Ch 4
</span>
<span class="etb-xref">
Ch 6
</span>
, taxes, tariffs, and quotas all shrink the pie the same way: they
<em>
interfere
</em>
with a market whose equilibrium was already efficient, blocking good trades and opening a deadweight-loss triangle. Call this the first family —
<strong>
distortions of a good equilibrium
</strong>
. The fix is usually to remove the interference.
</p>
<p>
But there's a second family coming in Unit 3, and it's stranger: cases where the market reaches its equilibrium smoothly, with no one blocking anything, and that equilibrium is
<em>
itself
</em>
the wrong one — because some cost or benefit isn't priced in (pollution, vaccines, education).
<span class="etb-xref">
Ch 19
</span>
There the deadweight loss exists even with no interference at all, and the fix may be to
<em>
add
</em>
a tax or subsidy rather than remove one. Keep the two families separate and the whole back half of this course stays clear:
<em>
is the market being prevented from working, or is it working fine and reaching a bad answer?
</em>
</p>
</div>
</div>
<details class="etb-retrieval">
<summary>
<span class="etb-ret-badge">
Retrieval practice
</span>
A tariff and a quota can be designed to produce the identical price, quantity, and deadweight loss. So what actually distinguishes them — and why does it matter?
<span class="etb-ret-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-ret-body">
<p>
The one difference is who captures the rectangle — the price gap times the imported quantity. Under a tariff the government collects it as revenue (a transfer that stays in the country and funds public spending). Under a quota the same value becomes quota rents, going to whoever holds the import licenses. That matters for two reasons. First, distribution: a tariff funds the treasury, while a quota enriches a private group — which is exactly why protected industries often lobby for quotas. Second, national welfare: if the license holders are foreign, the rectangle leaves the country entirely, so a quota with foreign-held licenses is strictly costlier than the equivalent tariff — the nation loses the two deadweight-loss triangles
<em>
and
</em>
the rectangle. Either way the deeper lesson holds: a tariff usually leaves the protecting country poorer because the loss to consumers exceeds the gains to producers and treasury combined, and the unrecovered gap is pure deadweight loss — trades with cheaper foreign sellers that simply stop happening.
<span class="etb-xref">
Ch 1
</span>
</p>
</div>
</details>
</div>
<!-- ⑤ CONCEPT 5 — TAX FAIRNESS & POLICY -->
<div class="etb-concept">
<div class="etb-slbl">
Major concept 5 · Who should pay
</div>
<h2>
Tax fairness and the limits of economics
</h2>
<div class="etb-body">
<p>
Everything so far has answered who
<em>
does
</em>
pay a tax and what it costs — pure positive economics, the same answer for any analyst who runs the numbers. Now the question that actually dominates every real tax fight: who
<em>
should
</em>
pay? That word —
<em>
should
</em>
— has crossed a line. It is a
<span data-tip="A claim about what ought to be — a value judgment.">
normative
</span>
question, and no supply-and-demand diagram answers it.
</p>
<p>
Start with what economics
<em>
can
</em>
say, because it's more than you'd guess. We can classify any tax by how its burden tracks income. A
<span data-tip="Takes a larger share of income as income rises.">
progressive
</span>
tax takes a bigger share of income the more you earn — the federal income tax, with its rising brackets. A
<span data-tip="Takes a larger share of income as income falls.">
regressive
</span>
tax takes a bigger share the
<em>
less
</em>
you earn — a flat sales tax is the classic case, because lower-income households spend a larger fraction of their income, so a tax on spending bites them harder. A
<span data-tip="Same share of income at every level.">
proportional
</span>
(or flat) tax takes the same share at every level. Which bucket a tax falls into is a factual, checkable question — positive economics; two experts will agree.
</p>
<p>
What economics
<em>
can't
</em>
settle is which of those is
<em>
fair
</em>
. Here two reasonable principles collide. The
<span data-tip="You should pay in proportion to the benefits you receive.">
benefit principle
</span>
says you should pay for what you use — a gas tax that funds the roads you drive on feels fair on those grounds. The
<span data-tip="Those more able to bear the burden should pay more.">
ability-to-pay principle
</span>
says the burden should fall where it's easiest to bear, which points toward progressive taxation, taking more from those who have more. Both are coherent; they often recommend opposite taxes; and nothing in economics crowns a winner, because the choice rests on values — fairness, desert, what we owe each other — that the model simply does not contain.
</p>
<p>
This is the discipline's honest boundary, worth stating plainly: economics is unusually powerful at the positive half — tracing a tax's consequences with real precision — and has no special authority over the normative half, whether those consequences are
<em>
good
</em>
. It's the same efficiency-versus-equity split we first drew in Chapter 5.
<span class="etb-xref">
Ch 5
</span>
A good citizen uses the economics to get the consequences right, then argues fairness openly, as the genuine values question it is. And this course returns to the case
<em>
for
</em>
taxes and redistribution, at full strength, later on.
<span class="etb-xref">
Ch 20
</span>
</p>
</div>
<details class="etb-misc">
<summary>
<span class="etb-misc-badge">
Common misconception
</span>
"A sales tax is the fairest tax — everyone pays the exact same rate."
<span class="etb-misc-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-misc-body">
<p>
The "same rate" is real, but it's the same rate on
<em>
spending
</em>
, not on
<em>
income
</em>
— and those come apart. A household earning $30,000 spends nearly all of it, so an 8% sales tax costs close to 8% of its income. A household earning $1,000,000 spends a far smaller share (the rest is saved and invested), so the same 8% costs only a sliver of its income. Identical rate, very different bite: the tax is
<em>
regressive
</em>
in income terms. That's a positive fact about who bears it. Whether regressive is "unfair" is the separate, normative argument — but you can't have that argument honestly until you see that "same rate" and "same burden" are not the same thing.
</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Quick check
</span>
A flat 8% sales tax — progressive, regressive, or proportional with respect to income?
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Regressive. Lower-income households spend a larger share of their income, so the tax takes a larger share of
<em>
their
</em>
income than of a high earner's. (Classification is positive; whether that's fair is normative.)
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Quick check
</span>
An income tax whose rate rises from 10% to 37% as income climbs through brackets — which structure?
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Progressive — a larger share of income is taken as income rises.
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-check">
<summary>
<span class="etb-check-tag">
Quick check
</span>
A flat $500 fee charged to every adult regardless of income — which structure?
<span class="etb-check-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-check-a">
Steeply regressive. A fixed dollar amount is a far larger share of a low income than a high one ($500 ÷ income falls as income rises).
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-retrieval">
<summary>
<span class="etb-ret-badge">
Retrieval practice
</span>
Why can economics tell you who bears a tax but not whether the tax is fair?
<span class="etb-ret-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-ret-body">
<p>
Because "who bears it" is a positive question — a fact about how prices and quantities adjust, settled by supply, demand, and elasticity, with an answer two analysts of opposite politics will share. "Is it fair?" is normative: it turns on values — what people deserve, what we owe one another, how to weigh one person's loss against another's gain — and those values are nowhere in the model. Economics can sharpen the fairness debate enormously by getting the consequences right (who actually pays, how much is destroyed, who's helped), but it cannot resolve it, because no arrangement of curves contains a definition of justice. Treating the positive answer as if it were the normative one — "the model says this tax is good" — is a category error, and a common one. The benefit principle and ability-to-pay are two honest, conflicting answers, and choosing between them is a job for citizens, not curves.
</p>
</div>
</details>
</div>
<!-- ⑥ ON THE EXAM -->
<div class="etb-exam">
<div class="etb-exam-head">
<span class="etb-exam-badge">
On the exam
</span>
<p class="etb-exam-title">
The moves you can now make
</p>
<p class="etb-exam-sub">
Four questions at the level the exam will actually ask — the tax wedge and its split, incidence from elasticity, revenue and deadweight loss, and a tariff worked end to end. Try each on paper first, then reveal the worked solution. The trying is where the learning happens.
</p>
</div>
<div class="etb-exam-tabs">
<button class="etb-exam-tab active" data-i="a">
