<div class="etb-exam-panel active" data-i="a">
<p class="etb-exam-type">
Type A · the wedge and the incidence split
</p>
<p class="etb-exam-q">
A market has demand P = 100 − 3Q and supply P = 40 + Q, clearing at $55 and 15 units (shown below). The government places an $8 per-unit tax on the good. Find the new quantity, the price buyers pay, the price sellers keep, and how the $8 splits between the two sides.
</p>
<div class="etb-figure" style="margin:.5rem 0 .9rem;">
<div class="etb-svgwrap">
<svg viewBox="0 0 600 360" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="A market clearing at 55 dollars and 15 units, with a tax shifting supply up and opening a wedge between the price buyers pay and the price sellers keep at a smaller quantity">
<line x1="70" y1="40" x2="70" y2="320" stroke="#1a1a1a" stroke-width="1.5">
</line>
<line x1="70" y1="320" x2="570" y2="320" stroke="#1a1a1a" stroke-width="1.5">
</line>
<text x="26" y="185" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" fill="#1a3a5c" text-anchor="middle" transform="rotate(-90 26 185)">
Price ($)
</text>
<text x="320" y="352" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" fill="#1a3a5c" text-anchor="middle">
Quantity
</text>
<g font-family="sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#777" text-anchor="end">
<text x="62" y="124">
61
</text>
<text x="62" y="154">
55
</text>
<text x="62" y="184">
53
</text>
</g>
<!-- demand P=100-3Q: from (70,40)[Q0 P100] to (390,320)[Q? ] schematic -->
<line x1="70" y1="70" x2="450" y2="320" stroke="#a33030" stroke-width="2.5">
</line>
<text x="456" y="320" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="#a33030">
D
</text>
<!-- supply P=40+Q -->
<line x1="70" y1="230" x2="500" y2="80" stroke="#0f7d7d" stroke-width="2.5">
</line>
<text x="504" y="78" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="#0f7d7d">
S
</text>
<!-- supply+tax (shifted up) -->
<line x1="70" y1="190" x2="430" y2="65" stroke="#0f7d7d" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 3">
</line>
<text x="434" y="63" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" fill="#0f7d7d">
S+tax
</text>
<!-- old eq at Q15 P55 (x=230) -->
<circle cx="230" cy="154" r="4" fill="#1a3a5c" opacity="0.5">
</circle>
<!-- new quantity Q13 (x=206); buyers 61 (y124) sellers 53 (y184) -->
<line x1="206" y1="124" x2="206" y2="320" stroke="#999" stroke-width="0.9" stroke-dasharray="3 3">
</line>
<line x1="70" y1="124" x2="206" y2="124" stroke="#a33030" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3">
</line>
<line x1="70" y1="184" x2="206" y2="184" stroke="#0f7d7d" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3">
</line>
<circle cx="206" cy="124" r="4.5" fill="#a33030">
</circle>
<circle cx="206" cy="184" r="4.5" fill="#0f7d7d">
</circle>
<text x="214" y="120" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" font-weight="700" fill="#a33030">
buyers pay $61
</text>
<text x="214" y="196" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" font-weight="700" fill="#0a4a4a">
sellers keep $53
</text>
<text x="206" y="335" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#777" text-anchor="middle">
13
</text>
<text x="230" y="335" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#777" text-anchor="middle" opacity="0.6">
15
</text>
</svg>
</div>
<div class="etb-figure-cap">
<span class="etb-figure-title">
The market with the $8 tax shifting supply up to S+tax.
</span>
</div>
</div>
<details class="etb-reveal">
<summary>
Show worked solution
<span class="etb-reveal-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-reveal-body">
<div class="etb-worked" style="margin:.3rem 0 0;">
<div class="etb-worked-body">
<ol class="etb-worked-steps">
<li>
The $8 tax lifts the price sellers need at every quantity: effective supply P = 40 + Q + 8 = 48 + Q.
</li>
<li>
New quantity: 100 − 3Q = 48 + Q → 52 = 4Q → Q = 13.
</li>
<li>
Price buyers pay (on demand): P = 100 − 3(13) = $61. Price sellers keep: $61 − $8 = $53.
</li>
<li>
Split against the old $55 price: buyers bear 61 − 55 = $6; sellers bear 55 − 53 = $2. (Check: 6 + 2 = 8 ✓.)
</li>
</ol>
<div class="etb-worked-answer">
New quantity 13; buyers pay $61, sellers keep $53. Buyers bear $6 of the $8, sellers $2 — demand is the steeper (less elastic) curve here, so the stuck side, buyers, carries more, whatever the law says about who remits it.
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</details>
</div>
<div class="etb-exam-panel" data-i="b">
<p class="etb-exam-type">
Type B · reading incidence off elasticity
</p>
<p class="etb-exam-q">
No equations — reason it out. (i) A state taxes cigarettes: demand is very inelastic (addictive, few substitutes), and sellers can redeploy shelf space to other products (elastic supply). Who bears most of the tax? (ii) A tax falls on a luxury watch with many close substitutes (elastic demand), made in specialized workshops that can't easily retool (inelastic supply). Who bears most of it? Explain the rule you're using.
</p>
<details class="etb-reveal">
<summary>
Show worked solution
<span class="etb-reveal-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-reveal-body">
<p>
<strong>
The rule:
</strong>
the burden falls on whichever side is more
<em>
inelastic
</em>
— the side that can least escape. The elastic side has alternatives and walks away; the stuck side swallows the tax to keep trading.
</p>
<p>
<strong>
(i)
</strong>
Buyers (smokers) bear most of it. Their demand is inelastic — they keep buying even as the price rises — while elastic sellers can shift to other goods. The tax passes through to consumers as a higher price. (This is also why cigarette taxes raise revenue reliably: the stuck goose doesn't fly.)
</p>
<p>
<strong>
(ii)
</strong>
The sellers (the workshops) bear most of it. Buyers have elastic demand — plenty of substitute watches — so they escape by buying something else; the specialized producers can't redeploy, so the burden comes out of their pocket as a lower price received. Same rule, opposite side: whoever is more stuck, pays more.
</p>
</div>
</details>
</div>
<div class="etb-exam-panel" data-i="c">
<p class="etb-exam-type">
Type C · revenue rectangle and deadweight-loss triangle
</p>
<p class="etb-exam-q">
A market has demand P = 80 − Q and supply P = 20 + Q (equilibrium $50, 30 units). A $10 per-unit tax is imposed, cutting the quantity to 25. Calculate the tax revenue and the deadweight loss, and explain in one line why the revenue is not itself a loss to society.
</p>
<details class="etb-reveal">
<summary>
Show worked solution
<span class="etb-reveal-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-reveal-body">
<div class="etb-worked" style="margin:.3rem 0 0;">
<div class="etb-worked-body">
<ol class="etb-worked-steps">
<li>
<strong>
Revenue (rectangle):
</strong>
tax per unit × quantity still sold = $10 × 25 = $250.
</li>
<li>
<strong>
Deadweight loss (triangle):
</strong>
base = lost trades = 30 − 25 = 5; height = the tax wedge = $10.
</li>
<li>
DWL = ½ × 5 × 10 = $25.
</li>
</ol>
<div class="etb-worked-answer">
Revenue = $250, deadweight loss = $25. The revenue isn't a loss because it is a
<em>
transfer
</em>
— surplus moved from buyers and sellers to the government, which spends it; the value still exists. The deadweight loss is the surplus on the 5 trades that no longer happen, which is destroyed and captured by no one.
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</details>
</div>
<div class="etb-exam-panel" data-i="d">
<p class="etb-exam-type">
Type D · the wedge at the border — a tariff
</p>
<p class="etb-exam-q">
A country's domestic demand is P = 120 − 2Q and domestic supply is P = 40 + 2Q. The world price is $60, and the country imports at that price. The government then imposes an $8 per-unit tariff (shown below). Find the domestic price, domestic production, domestic consumption, imports, government revenue, and the deadweight loss.
</p>
<div class="etb-figure" style="margin:.5rem 0 .9rem;">
<div class="etb-svgwrap">
<svg viewBox="0 0 600 360" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="A tariff raises the domestic price from a world price of 60 to 68, expanding domestic production and cutting consumption, shrinking imports, with a revenue rectangle and two deadweight-loss triangles">
<line x1="70" y1="40" x2="70" y2="320" stroke="#1a1a1a" stroke-width="1.5">
</line>
<line x1="70" y1="320" x2="570" y2="320" stroke="#1a1a1a" stroke-width="1.5">
</line>
<text x="26" y="185" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" fill="#1a3a5c" text-anchor="middle" transform="rotate(-90 26 185)">
Price ($)
</text>
<text x="320" y="352" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" fill="#1a3a5c" text-anchor="middle">
Quantity
</text>
<g font-family="sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#777" text-anchor="end">
<text x="62" y="154">
68
</text>
<text x="62" y="174">
60
</text>
</g>
<!-- demand P=120-2Q -->
<line x1="70" y1="60" x2="470" y2="320" stroke="#a33030" stroke-width="2.5">
</line>
<text x="476" y="320" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="#a33030">
D
</text>
<!-- supply P=40+2Q -->
<line x1="70" y1="240" x2="430" y2="60" stroke="#0f7d7d" stroke-width="2.5">
</line>
<text x="434" y="58" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="#0f7d7d">
S
</text>
<!-- world price 60 (y174) and tariff price 68 (y154) -->
<line x1="70" y1="174" x2="380" y2="174" stroke="#1a3a5c" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="6 3">
</line>
<text x="384" y="178" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" fill="#1a3a5c">
world price $60
</text>
<line x1="70" y1="154" x2="380" y2="154" stroke="#a33030" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="6 3">
</line>
<text x="384" y="150" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" fill="#a33030">
+ $8 tariff = $68
</text>
<!-- quantity guides: Qs1=10(x150), Qs2=14(x186), Qd2=26(x294), Qd1=30(x330) -->
<polygon points="186,174 222,174 222,154" fill="#2a7a4f" opacity="0.18">
</polygon>
<polygon points="150,174 186,174 186,154" fill="#c48b00" opacity="0.34">
</polygon>
<polygon points="222,174 258,174 222,154" fill="#c48b00" opacity="0.34">
</polygon>
<g font-family="sans-serif" font-size="9.5" fill="#777" text-anchor="middle">
<text x="150" y="334">
10
</text>
<text x="186" y="334">
14
</text>
<text x="222" y="334">
26
</text>
<text x="258" y="334">
30
</text>
</g>
<line x1="150" y1="174" x2="150" y2="320" stroke="#999" stroke-width="0.8" stroke-dasharray="3 3">
</line>
<line x1="186" y1="154" x2="186" y2="320" stroke="#999" stroke-width="0.8" stroke-dasharray="3 3">
</line>
<line x1="222" y1="154" x2="222" y2="320" stroke="#999" stroke-width="0.8" stroke-dasharray="3 3">
</line>
<line x1="258" y1="174" x2="258" y2="320" stroke="#999" stroke-width="0.8" stroke-dasharray="3 3">
</line>
</svg>
</div>
<div class="etb-figure-cap">
<span class="etb-figure-title">
The $8 tariff: domestic price rises to $68, production 10→14, consumption 30→26. Green = revenue; gold = the two deadweight-loss triangles.
</span>
</div>
</div>
<details class="etb-reveal">
<summary>
Show worked solution
<span class="etb-reveal-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-reveal-body">
<div class="etb-worked" style="margin:.3rem 0 0;">
<div class="etb-worked-body">
<ol class="etb-worked-steps">
<li>
<strong>
Free trade at $60:
</strong>
consumption 60 = 120 − 2Q → Q
<sub>
d
</sub>
= 30; production 60 = 40 + 2Q → Q
<sub>
s
</sub>
= 10; imports = 30 − 10 = 20.
</li>
<li>
<strong>
With the $8 tariff, price = $68:
</strong>
consumption 68 = 120 − 2Q → Q
<sub>
d
</sub>
= 26; production 68 = 40 + 2Q → Q
<sub>
s
</sub>
= 14; imports = 26 − 14 = 12.
</li>
<li>
<strong>
Revenue:
</strong>
tariff × imports = $8 × 12 = $96.
</li>
<li>
<strong>
Deadweight loss (two triangles):
</strong>
production side ½ × (14 − 10) × 8 = $16; consumption side ½ × (30 − 26) × 8 = $16. Total = $32.
</li>
</ol>
<div class="etb-worked-answer">
Domestic price $68; production 14, consumption 26, imports 12; revenue $96; deadweight loss $32. The tariff bends two margins — over-producing at home and under-consuming — so its loss is two triangles, not one. The green rectangle is revenue here; under a matched quota it would instead become rents to license holders.
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</details>
</div>
<!-- FRQ CAPSTONE (graph-bearing long form) -->
<div class="etb-frq">
<span class="etb-frq-badge">
Capstone · free response
</span>
<p class="etb-frq-title">
Put the moves together
</p>
<p class="etb-frq-meta">
AP-style long free response · ~10 points · graph required · model answer and scoring below
</p>
<p class="etb-frq-scenario">
A state legislature, looking for revenue, proposes a per-unit excise tax on soda. The market for soda is competitive, with demand P = 120 − 2Q and supply P = 30 + Q, where P is in cents per liter and Q is in millions of liters.
</p>
<ol class="etb-frq-parts">
<li>
Draw a correctly labeled supply-and-demand graph for soda, marking the equilibrium price (P
<sub>
1
</sub>
) and quantity (Q
<sub>
1
</sub>
).
<span class="etb-frq-pts">
(2 pts)
</span>
</li>
<li>
The state imposes a 12-cent per-liter tax. On your graph, show the tax wedge. Calculate the new quantity, the price buyers pay, and the price sellers keep.
<span class="etb-frq-pts">
(3 pts)
</span>
</li>
<li>
State how the 12-cent tax splits between buyers and sellers. Which side is more inelastic here, and how do you know?
<span class="etb-frq-pts">
(2 pts)
</span>
</li>
<li>
Calculate the government's tax revenue and the deadweight loss.
<span class="etb-frq-pts">
(2 pts)
</span>
</li>
<li>
Soda is consumed across all income levels, and lower-income households spend a larger share of income on it. Is this tax progressive or regressive with respect to income? Explain why that classification is a
<em>
positive
</em>
claim, while calling the tax "unfair" would be a
<em>
normative
</em>
one.
<span class="etb-frq-pts">
(1 pt)
</span>
</li>
</ol>
<details class="etb-reveal">
<summary>
Reveal a full-credit response
<span class="etb-reveal-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-reveal-body">
<p>
<strong>
(a)
</strong>
Vertical axis Price (cents/liter), horizontal axis Quantity (millions of liters), downward D and upward S crossing at E
<sub>
1
</sub>
, with P
<sub>
1
</sub>
and Q
<sub>
1
</sub>
marked. Equilibrium: 120 − 2Q = 30 + Q → 90 = 3Q → Q
<sub>
1
</sub>
= 30, P
<sub>
1
</sub>
= 60. (See figure.)
</p>
<p>
<strong>
(b)
</strong>
The 12-cent tax lifts effective supply to P = 30 + Q + 12 = 42 + Q, drawn as S+tax parallel above S. New quantity: 120 − 2Q = 42 + Q → 78 = 3Q → Q = 26. Price buyers pay (on demand): 120 − 2(26) = 68 cents. Price sellers keep: 68 − 12 = 56 cents. The wedge is the 12-cent vertical gap between the buyer price (68) and the seller price (56) at Q = 26.
</p>
<p>
<strong>
(c)
</strong>
Buyers bear 68 − 60 = 8 cents; sellers bear 60 − 56 = 4 cents. Buyers bear more. Demand is the more inelastic side here — its curve is steeper (slope −2 versus supply's +1), so buyers respond less to the price change and can least escape, carrying the larger share.
</p>
<p>
<strong>
(d)
</strong>
Revenue = tax × quantity sold = 12 × 26 = 312 (million cents = $3.12 million). Deadweight loss = ½ × (30 − 26) × 12 = ½ × 4 × 12 = 24 (million cents = $0.24 million).
</p>
<p>
<strong>
(e)
</strong>
Regressive: because lower-income households spend a larger share of their income on soda, a tax on soda takes a larger share of
<em>
their
</em>
income than of a high earner's. That classification is positive — a factual statement about how the burden tracks income, which any analyst can check. Calling the tax "unfair" is normative: it is a value judgment about whether that distribution of burden is acceptable, which the model cannot settle.
</p>
<div class="etb-figure" style="margin-top:.8rem;">
<div class="etb-svgwrap">
<svg viewBox="0 0 680 420" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Full-credit FRQ graph: soda demand and supply crossing at 60 cents and 30 units, a tax shifting supply up to open a 12-cent wedge at quantity 26, with buyers paying 68 and sellers keeping 56, and the deadweight-loss triangle shaded">
<line x1="80" y1="50" x2="80" y2="360" stroke="#1a1a1a" stroke-width="1.5">
</line>
<line x1="80" y1="360" x2="620" y2="360" stroke="#1a1a1a" stroke-width="1.5">
</line>
