E₁ (60, 30)
</text>
<!-- buyer & seller price markers at Q26 -->
<line x1="267" y1="146" x2="267" y2="360" stroke="#999" stroke-width="0.9" stroke-dasharray="3 3">
</line>
<line x1="80" y1="146" x2="267" y2="146" stroke="#a33030" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3">
</line>
<line x1="80" y1="218" x2="267" y2="218" stroke="#0f7d7d" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3">
</line>
<circle cx="267" cy="146" r="4.5" fill="#a33030">
</circle>
<circle cx="267" cy="218" r="4.5" fill="#0f7d7d">
</circle>
<text x="150" y="140" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" font-weight="700" fill="#a33030">
buyers pay 68
</text>
<text x="150" y="232" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" font-weight="700" fill="#0a4a4a">
sellers keep 56
</text>
<text x="330" y="186" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="9.5" fill="#8a6d2f">
12¢ wedge
</text>
<text x="267" y="376" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#777" text-anchor="middle">
26
</text>
<text x="296" y="376" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#777" text-anchor="middle" opacity="0.6">
30
</text>
</svg>
</div>
<div class="etb-figure-cap">
<span class="etb-figure-num">
Model graph
</span>
<span class="etb-figure-title">
Equilibrium 60¢ / 30 (part a); the 12¢ tax shifts supply to S+tax, cutting quantity to 26 and opening a wedge — buyers pay 68¢, sellers keep 56¢ (part b) — with the deadweight-loss triangle shaded (part d).
</span>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-reveal">
<summary>
Reveal the scoring rubric
<span class="etb-reveal-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-reveal-body">
<ul class="etb-rubric">
<li>
<span class="pt">
1 pt
</span>
(a) Axes labeled (Price vertical, Quantity horizontal) with a downward D and upward S.
</li>
<li>
<span class="pt">
1 pt
</span>
(a) Equilibrium marked at the crossing with P
<sub>
1
</sub>
= 60 and Q
<sub>
1
</sub>
= 30 (or shown on the axes).
</li>
<li>
<span class="pt">
1 pt
</span>
(b) Supply shown shifting up by the tax (S+tax), opening a wedge — not a movement along S.
</li>
<li>
<span class="pt">
1 pt
</span>
(b) Correct new quantity Q = 26 with work shown.
</li>
<li>
<span class="pt">
1 pt
</span>
(b) Buyer price 68 AND seller price 56.
</li>
<li>
<span class="pt">
1 pt
</span>
(c) Split stated: buyers 8, sellers 4.
</li>
<li>
<span class="pt">
1 pt
</span>
(c) Identifies demand as more inelastic AND justifies it (steeper curve / smaller response).
</li>
<li>
<span class="pt">
1 pt
</span>
(d) Tax revenue = 12 × 26 = 312 (with units).
</li>
<li>
<span class="pt">
1 pt
</span>
(d) Deadweight loss = ½ × 4 × 12 = 24, with work shown.
</li>
<li>
<span class="pt">
1 pt
</span>
(e) Regressive, AND explains the positive (classification) vs. normative (fairness) distinction.
</li>
</ul>
<p style="font-family:sans-serif;font-size:12px;color:#8a98a6;margin:.75rem 0 0;">
Ten independent points. A wrong number in (b) doesn't forfeit (c)–(d) if the later reasoning is correct given your own figures.
</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="etb-reveal">
<summary>
Reveal what loses points
<span class="etb-reveal-arrow">
▸
</span>
</summary>
<div class="etb-reveal-body">
<ul class="etb-loses">
<li>
<span class="tag" style="background:#fdecec;color:#a33030;">
Conceptual
</span>
In (c), claiming the side the law taxes "pays" the tax. Incidence is decided by elasticity, not the statute — here buyers bear more because demand is more inelastic, regardless of who remits the 12 cents.
</li>
<li>
<span class="tag" style="background:#fbf0db;color:#8a6d2f;">
Precision
</span>
In (d), forgetting the units (cents × millions of liters) or computing the DWL base as the new quantity (26) instead of the
<em>
lost
</em>
trades (30 − 26 = 4). The triangle's base is the quantity the tax killed.
</li>
<li>
<span class="tag" style="background:#eef2f6;color:#3e4e60;">
Exam craft
</span>
In (e), arguing whether the tax is fair instead of answering the question asked. The prompt wants the regressive classification (positive) and the positive-versus-normative distinction — sliding into your own fairness opinion earns nothing and can read as not understanding the line.
</li>
</ul>
</div>
</details>
</div>
</div>
<!-- ⑦ SYNTHESIS CARD -->
<div class="etb-synth">
<div class="etb-synth-eyebrow">
Market thesis
</div>
<p class="etb-synth-thesis">
A tax drives a wedge between what buyers pay and what sellers keep; its burden falls on whoever can least escape, and its cost splits into revenue the state collects and value destroyed outright. A tariff is the same wedge at the border.
</p>
<hr class="etb-synth-rule">
<div class="etb-synth-lead">
If economists ruled the world…
</div>
<p class="etb-synth-leadtext">
they would judge every tax by what it actually does — who truly bears it, and how much value it quietly destroys — not by whose name the law prints on the check.
</p>
<div class="etb-synth-cards">
<div class="etb-synth-card pos">
<div class="etb-synth-card-head">
<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#2a8a5a" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
<path d="M1 12s4-7 11-7 11 7 11 7-4 7-11 7-11-7-11-7z">
</path>
<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3">
</circle>
</svg>
<div>
<div class="etb-synth-card-label">
Positive
</div>
<div class="etb-synth-card-sub">
what economists agree on
</div>
</div>
</div>
<ul class="etb-synth-list">
<li>
A tax's burden lands on the more inelastic side, whatever the statute says — the law picks the check-writer, supply and demand pick the payer.
</li>
<li>
Every tax raises revenue (a transfer) and destroys some value (a deadweight-loss triangle); a tariff or quota is that same wedge applied at the border.
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="etb-synth-card norm">
<div class="etb-synth-card-head">
<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#b0883a" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
<path d="M12 4v16">
</path>
<path d="M5 20h14">
</path>
<path d="M4 7h16">
</path>
<path d="M4 7l-2.5 5h5z">
</path>
<path d="M20 7l-2.5 5h5z">
</path>
</svg>
<div>
<div class="etb-synth-card-label">
Normative
</div>
<div class="etb-synth-card-sub">
what citizens must decide
</div>
</div>
</div>
<ul class="etb-synth-list">
<li>
Who
<em>
ought
</em>
to pay — those who benefit, or those most able to bear it? Both principles are coherent and often point opposite ways.
</li>
<li>
Is a tax's revenue worth the value it destroys, and is the way its burden falls fair? Those are values questions the diagram can't decide.
</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<hr class="etb-synth-rule">
<div class="etb-synth-eyebrow">
Takeaways
</div>
<div class="etb-synth-takeaways">
<div class="etb-synth-take">
<div class="etb-synth-take-head">
<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#888" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
<path d="M20.8 4.6a5.5 5.5 0 0 0-7.8 0L12 5.7l-1-1.1a5.5 5.5 0 0 0-7.8 7.8l1 1.1L12 21l7.8-7.6 1-1a5.5 5.5 0 0 0 0-7.8z">
</path>
</svg>
In your life
</div>
<p>
When a tax or price jump lands on something you can't easily give up, you're the stuck side — and you'll bear it, whatever the label promises.
</p>
</div>
<div class="etb-synth-take">
<div class="etb-synth-take-head">
<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#888" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
<rect x="2" y="7" rx="2">
</rect>
<path d="M8 7V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h4a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v2">
</path>
</svg>
In your work
</div>
<p>
Pull any tax or trade barrier apart into the revenue it raises and the value it destroys, and ask which side is stuck before predicting who pays.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="etb-synth-arrow">
<span>
<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
<path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6">
</path>
</svg>
</span>
</div>
</div>
